														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4829170">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Wouldn't you agree that the majority of people who work for the Foundation fit this bill in some form or fashion?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Personally, no, I would definitely disagree with the statement. I would hope that the Foundation recruits in a manner that would result in the majority of their staff not having PTSD-triggering memories at all&#8212;counseling and therapy take up resources, and bring down employee morale overall. Granted, I've never been a fan of the grimdark approach to the Foundation, so if you want to write this for the portion of the audience that willingly believes that the majority of Foundation staff have dark pasts and traumatic memories, it's your prerogative.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The anomaly mind-melds the affected subjects into one mental being with two bodies, hence the final line. The resulting entity had all of the combined resentment of authority, trauma, and guilt from both individuals, and thus bided its time until it could dispatch Dr. Pollock effectively. Via the lip-reading section, this is implied to be retaliation for some unspeakable thing that occurred to SCP-XXXX-1B in the past.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For something like this, you'd probably be better off just putting it right in the description. An incident as significant as this in comparison with the conditions/states of the anomaly would be priority knowledge for anyone having to handle the anomaly. There's no point burying in a collapsed log with roundabout cryptic dialogue.</p>
<p>Although&#8230; as a reader, I'm still left with the &quot;so what?&quot; reaction. Why does the anomaly mind-meld people? How is the reader supposed to react to the kind of randomly added murder of Pollock, which seems kind of shoehorned in for shock value?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Isn't the fact that it can occur anywhere unpredictably enough to classify it as keter?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Does the Foundation have any confirmed evidence that the anomaly as observed in the Foundation staff occurs similarly elsewhere in the world? The way the article is set up, it seems solely focused on the one Foundation-based case, with no mention of the effects being noticed elsewhere. &quot;It could spontaneously occur anywhere&quot; and &quot;there could be more of these things out there&quot; aren't good enough to justify Keter designation if the in-universe Foundation is relying solely on extrapolation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

