														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4829164">
									

<p>Thank you for your feedback! I've put together some thoughts in response to your critiques that clear some things up if you'd like to take a look.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In reference to the inconsistencies you mentioned, I made the forum post before writing the sandbox, so I've flushed things out there that I didn't realize were vague in the post, with this aspect being one of them. Essentially there's an extremely quick deterioration of memory and records, so &quot;almost instantaneous&quot; aren't the correct words for it in hindsight, and I specify in the sandbox that it takes about 30 seconds from the incident to the time of total erasure, as if the snow globe is rapidly sucking their existence away.</p>
<p>A second distinction that I now realize is unclear in the post is that the snow globe itself has already been obtained by the Foundation when the MTF member disappears and the report is made. Essentially, the snow globe is constantly being recorded so that the foundation can study an isolated instance of human society, and as a result of this non-stop recording, it's able to capture the flare. The MTF team would not have been recorded themselves, but there would have been time to report the disappearance with the hinderance of specifics quickly evaporating from the minds of the witnesses. I'll take the time now to revise the forum post to clear up these discrepancies.</p>
<p>I do hear you on your D&amp;D rulebook entry analogy though, and I've had a few ideas floating in the back of my head on ways to enlarge the importance of the item itself. Notably, I've been considering including multiple instances of the snow globe that come into the possession of the Foundation, and the consequent discovery that the environments are linked to one another. I'm also open to constructing a narrative around someone who manages to find a way out, although I would have to take time to determine what life on the inside would truly be like.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

